<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kurdistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1922 CE – 1924 CE · capital Sulaymaniyah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Kurdish kingdom proclaimed at Sulaymaniyah by Sheikh Mahmud Barzanji in defiance of British plans for Iraq.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-barzanji">Sheikh Mahmud Barzanji</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> leader of the Qadiri order and notable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>, was appointed governor by the British in 1918. He rebelled in 1919, was defeated and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 1922, with Turkish forces threatening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> region, the British brought him back to restore order, and in November 1922 he proclaimed himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom had a cabinet, issued stamps and a newspaper, and claimed authority over the Kurdish districts around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>, but its administration was rudimentary and depended on tribal support. Mahmud negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> while the British sought to include the region in the new Iraqi state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> bombed Sulaymaniyah in 1923, one of the first uses of air power to police a colonial territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British and Iraqi forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a> in 1924, and the region was incorporated into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mandatory-iraq">Mandatory Iraq</a>. Sheikh Mahmud led further revolts until 1931 and remains a symbol of Kurdish nationalism.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kurdistan.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kurdistan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: FrancisTyers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kingdom_of_Kurdistan_(1922-1924).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1922 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sulaymaniyah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahmud Barzanji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish (Sorani)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sheikh Mahmud's Kurdistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1923&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kurdistan" aria-label="Kingdom of Kurdistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5828 893 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kurdistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6148 1149L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6148 1149h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6148 1149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sulaymaniyah. <a href="/atlas?year=1923&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kurdistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
